WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.271 --> 00:00:04.104
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:06.856
My is Evan Carmichael,
my one word is believe,

00:00:06.856 --> 00:00:08.254
and I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:08.254 --> 00:00:10.912
are going to solve all of
the worlds major problems.

00:00:10.912 --> 00:00:13.180
I started the Mentor Me series with a goal

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.149
to try to learn from people
who've done more than us,

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:18.163
and by hanging around
them a little bit longer,

00:00:18.163 --> 00:00:20.390
hopefully some of their
mindsets, their attitudes,

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.307
their beliefs, their
confidence seep into us

00:00:23.307 --> 00:00:26.495
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:26.495 --> 00:00:28.391
So today we're going to
learn from Joe Rogan,

00:00:28.391 --> 00:00:30.333
and some of his best motivation.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:32.199
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:34.518
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:34.518 --> 00:00:36.404
And as always guys, as
you watch these clips,

00:00:36.404 --> 00:00:38.547
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:38.547 --> 00:00:40.841
please leave it in quotes
in the comments below.

00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:44.394
You might win a prize, but
also if you write it down,

00:00:44.394 --> 00:00:47.596
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:47.596 --> 00:00:48.429
Enjoy.

00:00:49.461 --> 00:00:52.628
(inspirational music)

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.278
- You can get defined easily

00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:06.384
by other people's perceptions of you.

00:01:06.384 --> 00:01:09.611
My whole life I was convinced,
when I was a young man,

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:13.016
I was convinced I was a
loser, absolutely convinced.

00:01:13.016 --> 00:01:16.188
Until I started getting
really good at martial arts,

00:01:16.188 --> 00:01:18.345
I never thought I was good at anything.

00:01:18.345 --> 00:01:20.706
I just had a very low self-esteem,

00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:23.958
and I was limiting
myself by what I thought

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:26.329
were other people's opinions of me.

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:28.493
This is when I was a really young kid,

00:01:28.493 --> 00:01:31.090
and I didn't figure this
out until I was in my 30's.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.173
But I think that you
absolutely can be limited by

00:01:37.066 --> 00:01:40.012
your perceptions of
someone's definitions of you.

00:01:40.012 --> 00:01:43.051
But you can break through that stuff.

00:01:43.051 --> 00:01:46.606
That's what you break
through with discipline,

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.703
that's where you break
through with hard work

00:01:48.703 --> 00:01:50.185
and concentration and focus,

00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:52.457
and that's why it's so important

00:01:52.457 --> 00:01:55.469
to have either a discipline or an art

00:01:55.469 --> 00:01:56.893
or something that you're trying to create

00:01:56.893 --> 00:01:58.713
or something that you're
really focusing on,

00:01:58.713 --> 00:02:02.288
cause if you don't have a
point of focus as a human,

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:04.982
I think it's very hard
to get through this life

00:02:04.982 --> 00:02:08.109
and have an appreciation
for true struggle.

00:02:08.109 --> 00:02:09.391
Because our physical struggle

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:11.535
with what our bodies are designed for,

00:02:11.535 --> 00:02:14.181
the cavemen (bleep) 10,000 plus years ago,

00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:15.746
our bodies are still designed for that.

00:02:15.746 --> 00:02:18.402
That physical struggle
doesn't really manifest itself

00:02:18.402 --> 00:02:20.026
when you're sitting in front of a cubicle,

00:02:20.026 --> 00:02:22.339
you know, in front of a
computer in a cubicle,

00:02:22.339 --> 00:02:25.954
in this unnatural position all day.

00:02:25.954 --> 00:02:29.704
I think the whole
situation is very confusing

00:02:31.165 --> 00:02:35.332
for the human body, and we
don't get the tests that we need

00:02:36.385 --> 00:02:39.598
in order to have what you would
call personal sovereignty.

00:02:39.598 --> 00:02:42.429
So you've got to impose
those tests on yourself.

00:02:42.429 --> 00:02:44.603
Say if you had a kid,
obviously you do have a kid,

00:02:44.603 --> 00:02:48.580
but say if you had a 20 year
old and he's just a doper,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.893
where he wake and bakes,
doesn't get anything done,

00:02:50.893 --> 00:02:53.799
he's just always hanging
out with this friends

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:56.519
and playing video games
and he's just a loser.

00:02:56.519 --> 00:03:00.686
I wish there was a way you
could show someone like that,

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.242
I know that you're getting some comfort

00:03:04.242 --> 00:03:06.837
and satisfaction out of just
laying around, doing nothing,

00:03:06.837 --> 00:03:10.874
eating, getting fat, but
your life would feel better

00:03:10.874 --> 00:03:14.227
and richer if you had a
goal, you chased that goal,

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:15.189
you accomplished some things,

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:17.272
you would get this boost of confidence,

00:03:17.272 --> 00:03:18.904
you'd get this boost to self-esteem.

00:03:18.904 --> 00:03:20.242
Like, whatever it is
that you're into doing.

00:03:20.242 --> 00:03:21.746
Maybe you're into drawing comic books,

00:03:21.746 --> 00:03:25.465
maybe you're into making
pottery or sculptures,

00:03:25.465 --> 00:03:28.372
but find whatever the (censored) that is

00:03:28.372 --> 00:03:30.730
and pursue that instead of doing nothing.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:34.920
Like the people that are doing
nothing, those are the real,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.936
look, doing something might be as simple

00:03:36.936 --> 00:03:39.250
as that Alex Honald guy.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.704
He just climbs rocks,
but he's a world class

00:03:41.704 --> 00:03:42.659
rock climber.
- It's something though.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:43.657
- It's something.

00:03:43.657 --> 00:03:44.976
- I was always trying to learn,

00:03:44.976 --> 00:03:47.309
I was always trying to figure
out what I was doing wrong

00:03:47.309 --> 00:03:48.997
and what mistakes I was making,

00:03:48.997 --> 00:03:51.254
because if you don't do that,

00:03:51.254 --> 00:03:53.128
that's something I learned from jujitsu.

00:03:53.128 --> 00:03:56.332
If you don't do that, then
you're going to get passed by

00:03:56.332 --> 00:03:57.563
and other people are going to figure

00:03:57.563 --> 00:03:58.419
some new way of doing it

00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:00.282
and you're going to be left in the dark.

00:04:00.282 --> 00:04:04.326
- Yeah, as much as I like to
use the term 'that was perfect'

00:04:04.326 --> 00:04:06.876
there really is nothing
perfect in human beings.

00:04:06.876 --> 00:04:08.691
There's always room for improvement,

00:04:08.691 --> 00:04:10.186
there's always a shorter path,

00:04:10.186 --> 00:04:11.561
there's always a quicker victory,

00:04:11.561 --> 00:04:14.478
there's always new things to learn.

00:04:16.369 --> 00:04:18.039
As soon as you start thinking

00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:22.886
that you've mastered something
to the point of an end,

00:04:22.886 --> 00:04:25.341
you've kind of missed
out what it's all about

00:04:25.341 --> 00:04:27.275
in the first place.

00:04:27.275 --> 00:04:28.523
You're constantly uncomfortable,

00:04:28.523 --> 00:04:30.213
you're supposed to be
constantly uncomfortable,

00:04:30.213 --> 00:04:32.139
and these little victories that you get,

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:33.502
the good thing about when people tap,

00:04:33.502 --> 00:04:35.898
you get aaah, you get a little
nice feeling right here.

00:04:35.898 --> 00:04:37.600
- Yeah, and then you're
like, let's go again.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.577
- And then you're like, ah (censored),

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:40.121
back to being uncomfortable.

00:04:40.121 --> 00:04:42.367
There's no getting, and then he taps you,

00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:43.850
and you're like, ah (censored).

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.036
Should have quit while I was ahead.

00:04:45.036 --> 00:04:47.172
No, no, because you're missing the point.

00:04:47.172 --> 00:04:49.940
The point is that it's a long path,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.561
a long arduous path, and I think
anything that's worth doing

00:04:53.561 --> 00:04:55.553
is probably like that.

00:04:55.553 --> 00:04:58.604
Yeah, right or wrong,
I'm real as I can be.

00:04:58.604 --> 00:05:01.247
If I'm wrong, I'm sorry, I
didn't think I was wrong.

00:05:01.247 --> 00:05:03.383
I'll tell you if I'm wrong,

00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:06.995
but I'mma be as honest
about this whole thing

00:05:06.995 --> 00:05:09.875
as I possibly can, and I
think we owe each other that,

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:11.798
and I think the idea that we don't

00:05:11.798 --> 00:05:14.531
and that we shouldn't think that deeply,

00:05:14.531 --> 00:05:17.121
or we shouldn't uncover
problems and issues

00:05:17.121 --> 00:05:20.302
and we should just go about our lives,

00:05:20.302 --> 00:05:22.064
ooh, I don't think that makes sense.

00:05:22.064 --> 00:05:24.291
I don't think that makes
sense mathematically,

00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:25.987
it doesn't make sense logically.

00:05:25.987 --> 00:05:30.512
I think we, as a species,
owe it to ourselves

00:05:30.512 --> 00:05:32.040
to be honest with ourselves.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.664
That's the best benefit that we get

00:05:33.664 --> 00:05:35.120
from being around other people,

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.344
is to understand the real
information as they see it,

00:05:39.344 --> 00:05:41.325
and you can process that any way you like.

00:05:41.325 --> 00:05:44.015
You can put it through the filter of ego

00:05:44.015 --> 00:05:46.539
and personal circumstance
and all sorts of things

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:48.371
that make people have opinions

00:05:48.371 --> 00:05:50.100
that are biased in one way or another,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.712
whether it's liberal or republican,

00:05:51.712 --> 00:05:54.059
but when you get past all that,

00:05:54.059 --> 00:05:57.892
you really get to see
how a person is thinking

00:05:59.557 --> 00:06:01.032
when they're honest.

00:06:01.032 --> 00:06:03.343
And when they're not,
you don't really know

00:06:03.343 --> 00:06:04.552
who you're talking to.

00:06:04.552 --> 00:06:07.818
You're dealing in this
fantasy world of (bleep).

00:06:07.818 --> 00:06:08.983
You're dealing in this world

00:06:08.983 --> 00:06:11.104
where someone's pretending to be something

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:13.268
because it looks like that
looks best on the numbers.

00:06:13.268 --> 00:06:15.195
You know, they've looked at some sheets

00:06:15.195 --> 00:06:18.722
and say they prefer
Christian, nothing Mormon

00:06:18.722 --> 00:06:20.979
or anything crazy like that,
that's what people prefer,

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:22.242
so let's go Christian.

00:06:22.242 --> 00:06:23.905
They prefer a person that wears

00:06:23.905 --> 00:06:25.490
a tie and a suit all the time

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.562
so let's dress in the appropriate manner,

00:06:27.562 --> 00:06:32.035
and they sort of slowly but
surely segment themselves

00:06:32.035 --> 00:06:35.570
into this ridiculous
population of pretenders.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.326
I'm fascinated by martial arts.

00:06:37.326 --> 00:06:39.108
I'm fascinated by comedy.

00:06:39.108 --> 00:06:42.150
I'm fascinated by many,
many different things.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.558
I don't understand when
people say they're bored,

00:06:44.558 --> 00:06:47.672
because if I had the time
to live a hundred lives,

00:06:47.672 --> 00:06:49.520
I'd be speaking different languages,

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.110
I'd be living in different countries,

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.408
I would try a number of different careers,

00:06:53.408 --> 00:06:56.474
'cause I think there's a lot
of unbelievably fascinating,

00:06:56.474 --> 00:07:00.092
puzzling, complex things that
you could study in this world.

00:07:00.092 --> 00:07:02.090
But that's just me and my personality,

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:03.879
but that's the personality
also that I've cultivated

00:07:03.879 --> 00:07:05.603
over years of challenges.
- Kind of like that

00:07:05.603 --> 00:07:06.695
as a kid too.

00:07:06.695 --> 00:07:08.327
- Well I was involve din
martial arts very early

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.322
and I think that is one of the things

00:07:10.322 --> 00:07:14.784
that motivated me to
explore difficult tasks

00:07:14.784 --> 00:07:16.810
because through difficult tasks

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.266
you learn an incredible
amount about yourself,

00:07:19.266 --> 00:07:22.266
and through the fire of competition,

00:07:23.266 --> 00:07:26.016
you get to understand motivation,

00:07:27.218 --> 00:07:29.510
you get to understand the
resistance that you have

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.546
inside your mind to doing hard work,

00:07:31.546 --> 00:07:33.870
you get to understand the
rewards of discipline.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.360
You don't truly appreciate relaxation

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.902
unless you've worked hard,

00:07:38.902 --> 00:07:41.100
and that is the yin and the yang of life.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.756
I've said this to the point
of people getting sick of it,

00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:48.919
but one of the worst
decisions a man can make,

00:07:48.919 --> 00:07:52.662
I can only speak for men
obviously, is to be comfortable.

00:07:52.662 --> 00:07:55.023
I don't think you should
try to be comfortable.

00:07:55.023 --> 00:07:58.462
I think what you should try
to do is try to earn comfort,

00:07:58.462 --> 00:08:02.317
and if you can get a day
off where you've worked hard

00:08:02.317 --> 00:08:04.388
and you've accomplished goals,

00:08:04.388 --> 00:08:06.061
that day off will be so sweet.

00:08:06.061 --> 00:08:08.172
When I work hard and I
sit in front of the T.V.,

00:08:08.172 --> 00:08:09.219
I enjoy the shit out of it.

00:08:09.219 --> 00:08:12.472
I put my feet up, I have a nice drink,

00:08:12.472 --> 00:08:13.417
I enjoy my free time.
- Do you have one of those

00:08:13.417 --> 00:08:14.678
chairs that kneads your back

00:08:14.678 --> 00:08:15.511
or something like that?
- I do have one of those.

00:08:15.511 --> 00:08:16.932
- Do you really?
- Those are great.

00:08:16.932 --> 00:08:19.295
I don't use it that much though, honestly.

00:08:19.295 --> 00:08:23.333
I'm more of a workaholic
than I should be, probably.

00:08:23.333 --> 00:08:25.582
I probably should relax more than I do,

00:08:25.582 --> 00:08:27.340
but I never feel like I earned it.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.613
But that's part of the reason why

00:08:28.613 --> 00:08:30.989
when I do feel like I
earned it, I can enjoy it.

00:08:30.989 --> 00:08:34.572
It's 'cause I am more
connected to the idea

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.369
that I need to accomplish things.

00:08:38.369 --> 00:08:43.028
It's not for anybody else's
benefit other than my own,

00:08:43.028 --> 00:08:45.573
or anybody else's approval
other than my own.

00:08:45.573 --> 00:08:47.867
When I have a task, whether it's,

00:08:47.867 --> 00:08:51.705
today I'm going to write
1000 words, or 2000,

00:08:51.705 --> 00:08:52.538
or whatever the number is.

00:08:52.538 --> 00:08:55.417
If I don't that, I write things down.

00:08:55.417 --> 00:08:56.500
I'll write down a list of things

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:57.980
that I'll get accomplished that day,

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.179
and if I don't accomplish
that, I'll get sick.

00:09:00.179 --> 00:09:02.052
I'll drive me (censored) crazy.

00:09:02.052 --> 00:09:05.233
If I can't fill out that
list, that drives me nuts.

00:09:05.233 --> 00:09:09.107
That's what led me to
be a championship level

00:09:09.107 --> 00:09:11.990
martial artist, that's
what led me to achieve,

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.157
it's the reinforcement of those goals.

00:09:17.284 --> 00:09:20.202
Understanding that you
can achieve those goals,

00:09:20.202 --> 00:09:21.050
it's going to be difficult,

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.424
you're going to push
through the difficulty,

00:09:22.424 --> 00:09:24.964
and then you're going to understand
what difficulty truly is,

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:26.342
and how much of it is just mental,

00:09:26.342 --> 00:09:27.719
how much of it is just in your mind,

00:09:27.719 --> 00:09:31.886
this adversity to a difficult
task or to struggle.

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:35.150
A lot of people have that, they're scared.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.075
They're scared of complications,

00:09:37.075 --> 00:09:39.658
they're scared of failure.

00:09:39.658 --> 00:09:41.348
Failure's a big one that
people are afraid of.

00:09:41.348 --> 00:09:43.092
But failure's one of the
most important things

00:09:43.092 --> 00:09:45.496
you could ever have as
far as the motivation

00:09:45.496 --> 00:09:46.761
to do things differently.

00:09:46.761 --> 00:09:50.297
One of the reasons why
I think that I'm good

00:09:50.297 --> 00:09:52.476
at friendships and relationships

00:09:52.476 --> 00:09:54.975
is because I've failed at them in past.

00:09:54.975 --> 00:09:56.604
One of the things that I'm good at comedy,

00:09:56.604 --> 00:09:58.072
is because I've bombed on stage.

00:09:58.072 --> 00:10:00.151
One of the reasons why I'm good at work

00:10:00.151 --> 00:10:03.116
is because I've been a
(censored) worker in the past

00:10:03.116 --> 00:10:05.362
and I know the feeling of failure,

00:10:05.362 --> 00:10:09.035
the feeling of shame, of being a weak,

00:10:09.035 --> 00:10:11.441
non-motivated, lazy person.

00:10:11.441 --> 00:10:13.110
That's a weak feeling.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.705
You don't respect yourself.

00:10:14.705 --> 00:10:17.405
And I have this phrase that
I use all the time to people

00:10:17.405 --> 00:10:18.980
to try to motivate people.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:21.794
I say that, be the hero in your own movie.

00:10:21.794 --> 00:10:23.546
Pretend that if your life was a movie

00:10:23.546 --> 00:10:25.793
and your life started now,
what would the hero do?

00:10:25.793 --> 00:10:27.411
What would the person that you respect do?

00:10:27.411 --> 00:10:28.367
What would the person that you admire,

00:10:28.367 --> 00:10:30.440
person that inspires
you, what would they do?

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:32.490
Well do that (censored),
and if you do that,

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.479
you slowly build momentum.

00:10:34.479 --> 00:10:37.361
You're like, today I
did what I wanted to do.

00:10:37.361 --> 00:10:40.670
Today I started a class
in yoga, I did this,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:42.611
I did all these things that I
was saying I wasn't going to do

00:10:42.611 --> 00:10:44.199
and now I feel momentum,

00:10:44.199 --> 00:10:48.303
and momentum is a very important
point in people's lives.

00:10:48.303 --> 00:10:50.169
That's why some folks
don't like to take days off

00:10:50.169 --> 00:10:52.259
because they feel like
they're losing momentum,

00:10:52.259 --> 00:10:54.405
and they sort of have to
restart the wheel up again

00:10:54.405 --> 00:10:56.043
after a vacation.

00:10:56.043 --> 00:10:58.009
When you're alone with your thoughts,

00:10:58.009 --> 00:11:00.329
you get an idea of what
your thoughts actually are.

00:11:00.329 --> 00:11:01.987
If you life your life
just acting constantly

00:11:01.987 --> 00:11:04.620
on the momentum of other
people's expectations,

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.522
of you wanting to be liked
by these other people,

00:11:07.522 --> 00:11:11.630
you can run into a trap,
and you set up a life

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.230
that you didn't really want.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:17.267
You're trapped in this situation
where you have a mortgage,

00:11:17.267 --> 00:11:19.632
you've got credit card
bills, you got student loans

00:11:19.632 --> 00:11:21.679
you have to pay, you have a
bunch of (censored) going on

00:11:21.679 --> 00:11:24.676
that you have to continue
to feed and all that,

00:11:24.676 --> 00:11:25.790
and especially if you have a family

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:27.646
and you have to feed them, oh my goodness.

00:11:27.646 --> 00:11:29.083
Then you're fully locked in.

00:11:29.083 --> 00:11:31.157
You can't take any chances whatsoever.

00:11:31.157 --> 00:11:35.807
An often-times people make
the mistake of getting stuck,

00:11:35.807 --> 00:11:37.893
and it is just a tactical mistake,

00:11:37.893 --> 00:11:39.859
just like it would be a
mistake if you got stuck

00:11:39.859 --> 00:11:42.487
on a video game, just
like it would be a mistake

00:11:42.487 --> 00:11:44.937
if you followed a map incorrectly

00:11:44.937 --> 00:11:46.578
and you got stuck in the woods.

00:11:46.578 --> 00:11:49.226
Your life is certainly
some sort of a journey.

00:11:49.226 --> 00:11:50.838
It's certainly some sort of a journey,

00:11:50.838 --> 00:11:53.726
and we have to all be aware
that when we're making journeys,

00:11:53.726 --> 00:11:56.211
we're not going to always
make the right steps,

00:11:56.211 --> 00:11:58.156
and sometimes you have
to back up and try again,

00:11:58.156 --> 00:11:59.564
and if you're in a position
where you can't back up

00:11:59.564 --> 00:12:02.380
and try again, you've trapped yourself,

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.546
and the system will set out honey pots

00:12:05.546 --> 00:12:07.201
for people to get trapped in.

00:12:07.201 --> 00:12:10.151
The system will set out
the ideas of retirement,

00:12:10.151 --> 00:12:12.833
the ideas of the golden
years, providing you benefits,

00:12:12.833 --> 00:12:14.944
providing you a healthy work environment.

00:12:14.944 --> 00:12:15.777
Why?

00:12:15.777 --> 00:12:17.032
Well because they want
people to work for them.

00:12:17.032 --> 00:12:19.479
They don't want people to
realize their own dreams

00:12:19.479 --> 00:12:21.873
and escape, well that's a pain in the ass,

00:12:21.873 --> 00:12:24.037
you got to hire more
people and train them.

00:12:24.037 --> 00:12:26.899
They want to set it up
so that you stick around.

00:12:26.899 --> 00:12:29.100
You stick around in some sort
of an unsatisfying world.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:32.300
It's up to you to see
that video game problem,

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:34.262
to see that issue as
it comes up on the map.

00:12:34.262 --> 00:12:35.606
No, no, I think this is a right turn,

00:12:35.606 --> 00:12:38.967
to see all the problems
that could potentially

00:12:38.967 --> 00:12:41.963
lay in front of you, and
calculate your future.

00:12:41.963 --> 00:12:43.475
And then also look around all the people

00:12:43.475 --> 00:12:45.491
that didn't do it and look
at the misery they're in,

00:12:45.491 --> 00:12:47.341
and learn that you don't
want to be like them.

00:12:47.341 --> 00:12:50.314
And then look at the people
have kind of tooken chances

00:12:50.314 --> 00:12:51.590
and navigated their way.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:53.065
What did they do differently than you?

00:12:53.065 --> 00:12:55.985
What objectivity do they
have that maybe you lack.

00:12:55.985 --> 00:12:57.447
What insight into their own mistakes

00:12:57.447 --> 00:13:00.620
are they willing to delve
into that you're not,

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:02.033
that you step back and you go,

00:13:02.033 --> 00:13:05.369
I just don't want to look
at myself that closely.

00:13:05.369 --> 00:13:08.055
But the person that's able to
look at themselves the closest

00:13:08.055 --> 00:13:10.401
is going to get the more rational results.

00:13:10.401 --> 00:13:12.192
And there's a lot of folks that live life

00:13:12.192 --> 00:13:14.774
on a cushy cloud of
marshmallows and (censored),

00:13:14.774 --> 00:13:16.112
and then one day something goes wrong,

00:13:16.112 --> 00:13:19.482
and I mean, that's why
spoiled kids are so sad.

00:13:19.482 --> 00:13:22.635
Like, a spoiled young boy is
one of the saddest things ever.

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:24.143
A young boy that becomes a man

00:13:24.143 --> 00:13:25.849
and can't take care of himself,

00:13:25.849 --> 00:13:28.089
and his dad has to keep on rescuing him,

00:13:28.089 --> 00:13:30.648
his dad has to keep on
bailing him out of situations

00:13:30.648 --> 00:13:31.499
and giving him money.

00:13:31.499 --> 00:13:34.740
I've met guys like that, and
that is a crippling affliction

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.770
when they don't have
the character themselves

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.220
to be able to get by in life.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.854
They constantly need someone to help them

00:13:40.854 --> 00:13:41.687
and bail them out.

00:13:41.687 --> 00:13:44.402
Even as a grown man, I've
met guys in their 40's

00:13:44.402 --> 00:13:46.974
that still need help from their parents.

00:13:46.974 --> 00:13:49.014
I'm like what the (censored) man?

00:13:49.014 --> 00:13:50.638
You're never going to get it right

00:13:50.638 --> 00:13:52.424
because somewhere along the line

00:13:52.424 --> 00:13:54.152
they didn't face enough of the adversity

00:13:54.152 --> 00:13:56.542
to realize that there's some
times we just have to get up

00:13:56.542 --> 00:13:57.857
and get (censored) done.

00:13:57.857 --> 00:13:59.394
There's some times where
you have to (censored)

00:13:59.394 --> 00:14:02.162
pull yourself up and you
have to push forward,

00:14:02.162 --> 00:14:03.782
even if you want to stay in bed.

00:14:03.782 --> 00:14:04.725
And if you don't do that,

00:14:04.725 --> 00:14:06.197
and you just keep calling on your Daddy,

00:14:06.197 --> 00:14:07.455
and your Daddy keeps rescuing you,

00:14:07.455 --> 00:14:08.963
you never develop those tools.

00:14:08.963 --> 00:14:11.343
You never develop that
ability to recognize

00:14:11.343 --> 00:14:12.953
what you're doing wrong with your life.

00:14:12.953 --> 00:14:14.894
'Cause you're soft, you got a cushion,

00:14:14.894 --> 00:14:17.229
you got a safety net, the
safety net for your safety net.

00:14:17.229 --> 00:14:19.117
You know it sounds really hippy,

00:14:19.117 --> 00:14:22.129
but true happiness comes from
making other people happy.

00:14:22.129 --> 00:14:24.544
True happiness comes from
being around happy people

00:14:24.544 --> 00:14:26.522
and enjoying each other's company.

00:14:26.522 --> 00:14:29.366
That's really hard for people
to wrap their heads around,

00:14:29.366 --> 00:14:31.737
because they always
associate true happiness,

00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:35.570
no true happiness is
with titles, or numbers,

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:37.947
or objects that you possess.

00:14:37.947 --> 00:14:40.353
You're not concerned about
being nice to people,

00:14:40.353 --> 00:14:41.534
you're not concerned about

00:14:41.534 --> 00:14:43.354
having a positive impact on people.

00:14:43.354 --> 00:14:45.722
You're more concerned with
making ones and zeros.

00:14:45.722 --> 00:14:46.962
That creates a negative
- And when you don't care

00:14:46.962 --> 00:14:48.514
about other people, doesn't it really say

00:14:48.514 --> 00:14:49.589
that you don't care about yourself?

00:14:49.589 --> 00:14:51.935
- It does, and see we're not taught that.

00:14:51.935 --> 00:14:55.296
It sounds like hippy (censored),
but it really truly is

00:14:55.296 --> 00:14:58.071
how the human race
interacts with each other.

00:14:58.071 --> 00:15:02.116
You really have to be nice
to other people to be happy.

00:15:02.116 --> 00:15:03.279
You have to.

00:15:03.279 --> 00:15:05.008
If you're not, you won't be happy.

00:15:05.008 --> 00:15:07.841
We all make mistakes and
we all find ourselves

00:15:07.841 --> 00:15:09.919
in positions of frustration

00:15:09.919 --> 00:15:12.531
and we've all acted out, yelled at our dog

00:15:12.531 --> 00:15:14.074
when maybe we shouldn't have.

00:15:14.074 --> 00:15:16.643
There's pressures in this life.

00:15:16.643 --> 00:15:18.654
But I think it's really
important to recognize

00:15:18.654 --> 00:15:22.321
what those truly are,
and to understand that

00:15:23.211 --> 00:15:27.269
at the end of it, we can alleviate a lot

00:15:27.269 --> 00:15:30.295
of how we deal with this life,

00:15:30.295 --> 00:15:34.772
if we really treated people
the way we would treat them,

00:15:34.772 --> 00:15:37.541
as if they were ourselves
living another life.

00:15:37.541 --> 00:15:39.241
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

